==========================================pipes=====================================================================================
Pipe = a function that transforms data for display in the template
👉 Pipes do NOT change the original data
👉 They are used only in templates
👉 Syntax uses the pipe operator |

Simple example
<p>{{ userName | uppercase }}</p>
userName = 'nazeer';
Output
NAZEER
✔ Original userName is still 'nazeer'

============================================================================================================================
Why Pipes Exist?
Without pipes:
<p>{{ userName.toUpperCase() }}</p>
Problems:
❌ Logic inside template
❌ Hard to reuse
❌ Poor readability
❌ Difficult to test
✔ Pipes solve this by separating transformation logic
=================================================================================================================
Types of:
1. String pipes
  uppercase
  {{ 'angular' | uppercase }}
  lowercase
  {{ 'ANGULAR' | lowercase }}
  titlecase
  {{ 'angular developer' | titlecase }}
  ➡ Angular Developer

2.Number pipes
  number 
  {{ 12345.6789 | number:'1.2-2' }}
  ➡ 12,345.68
  '1.2-2'
   ↑ ↑ ↑
   | | |
   | | └─ maxFractionDigits = 2
   | └── minFractionDigits = 2
   └──── minIntegerDigits = 1

percentage
  {{ 0.25 | percent }}
  ➡ 25%

currency
  {{ 1500 | currency:'INR':'symbol' }}
  ➡ ₹1,500.00
  {{ 1500 | currency:'USD':'symbol' }}
  ➡ $1,500.00

date pipe
  {{ today | date:'dd-MM-yyyy' }}
  today = new Date();
  Common formats:
  Format	    Output
  short	      1/1/25, 3:00 PM
  mediumDate	Jan 1, 2025
  longDate	  January 1, 2025
  dd/MM/yyyy	01/01/2025

JSON Pipe
  Very useful for debugging 👌
  <pre>{{ user | json }}</pre>
  user = { name: 'Nazeer', role: 'Angular Dev' };
  ➡
  {
    "name": "Nazeer",
    "role": "Angular Dev"
  }

Slice Pipe
  Works like Array.slice() or String.slice()
  {{ 'Angular' | slice:0:3 }}
  ➡ Ang
  {{ users | slice:0:2 }}

6. Async Pipe (VERY IMPORTANT ⭐)
  Used with:
  ✔ Observables
  ✔ Promises
  {{ users$ | async }}
  Behind the scenes:
  Subscribes automatically
  Updates UI on emission
  Unsubscribes automatically
  ➡ Prevents memory leaks

🔹 7. KeyValue Pipe
  Used for objects
  <div *ngFor="let item of user | keyvalue">
    {{ item.key }} : {{ item.value }}
  </div>

================================================================================================================
Almost all predefined Angular pipes are pure pipes.
Only AsyncPipe behaves like an impure pipe (but internally optimized).

1️⃣ What does pure vs impure mean?
✅ Pure Pipe
  Executes only when input reference changes
  Very performance friendly
  Default behavior in Angular
  @Pipe({ name: 'myPipe', pure: true }) // default

❌ Impure Pipe
  Executes on every change detection cycle
  Can be expensive
  Must be explicitly marked
  @Pipe({ name: 'myPipe', pure: false })
=========================================================================================================================
Creating a Custom Pipe (Pure Pipe)

Example: Capitalize First Letter
ng generate pipe capitalize
@Pipe({
  name: 'capitalize'
})
export class CapitalizePipe implements PipeTransform {

  transform(value: string): string {
    if (!value) return value;
    return value.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + value.slice(1);
  }
}

Usage
{{ 'angular' | capitalize }}
➡ Angular

7️⃣ Custom Pipe with Arguments
transform(value: string, limit: number): string {
  return value.slice(0, limit);
}
{{ 'AngularDeveloper' | custom:7 }}
➡ Angular

8️⃣ Impure Custom Pipe Example (NOT Recommended)
@Pipe({
  name: 'filterUsers',
  pure: false
})
export class FilterUsersPipe implements PipeTransform {

  transform(users: string[], search: string): string[] {
    return users.filter(u => u.includes(search));
  }
}
⚠️ This runs on every keystroke / CD cycle
✔ Prefer filtering in component or using signals/RxJS

9️⃣ Pipes vs Methods in Template (Interview Question)
❌ Method in template:
{{ getTotal() }}
Runs every CD cycle
✔ Pipe:
{{ total | calculate }}
Runs only when input changes


